    <h1>GDPR EU Representative: What Article 27 Requires and How to Comply</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published September 19, 2026</p>

    <p>A GDPR EU representative is a person or organization based in the European Union that a non-EU company designates as its local contact point for data protection authorities and individuals whose data it processes. Article 27 of the GDPR requires this appointment whenever a company outside the EU is subject to the regulation under Article 3(2) -- meaning it offers goods or services to people in the EU, or monitors their behavior. If your app or website serves EU users and you have no EU establishment, you almost certainly need one.</p>

    <p>This guide covers what Article 27 actually says, who qualifies for an exemption, how a representative differs from a Data Protection Officer, what the appointment process involves, and how to disclose your representative in your privacy policy with ready-to-use clause text. If you are not yet sure whether the GDPR applies to your company at all, start with our breakdown of <a href="/resources/does-gdpr-apply-to-us-companies/">whether GDPR applies to US companies</a> -- it covers the Article 3 territorial-scope test in detail.</p>

